{"park":"north_cascades","fetched":"2026-09-06T23:39:38+00:00","attribution":{"landmarks":"© OpenStreetMap contributors, ODbL","summaries":"Wikipedia, CC BY-SA 4.0","boundary":"iNaturalist place 69097"},"landmarks":[{"id":"node/7888619481","name":"Depot Creek Falls","kind":"waterfall","lon":-121.28475,"lat":48.97772,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q37513","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Depot_Creek_Falls","tour":2,"summary":{"extract":"Depot Creek Falls is a 967-foot (295 m)-high waterfall in the North Cascades National Park, Whatcom County, Washington.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Depot_Creek_Falls","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"},"photos":[],"street":null},{"id":"node/7921205343","name":"Sulphide Creek Falls","kind":"waterfall","lon":-121.57615,"lat":48.79826,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q37249","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sulphide_Creek_Falls","tour":0,"summary":{"extract":"Sulphide Creek Falls is a tall, moderately large volume waterfall within North Cascades National Park in Washington state that is one of the tallest waterfalls in North America. The falls drop from Sulphide Lake on the southeast side of Mount Shuksan down a narrow flume-like canyon to a broad basin below. Because of the narrow, twisting shape of the canyon the waterfall is exceptionally difficult to see from ground-level perspectives. The total vertical drop of the waterfall is in the range of 2,100 feet (640 m) to 2,200 feet (670 m) feet, but it has not yet been accurately measured. Foot access to the bottom of the waterfall involves 2.5 miles (4.0 km) of off-trail travel in extremely brushy terrain and several potentially dangerous fords of a large stream.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sulphide_Creek_Falls","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"},"photos":[{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Sulphide_Creek_Falls.jpg/960px-Sulphide_Creek_Falls.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sulphide_Creek_Falls.jpg","dist_m":106,"width":640,"height":1138,"license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","artist":"Antrocent"}],"street":null},{"id":"node/1942197916","name":"Berdeen Falls","kind":"waterfall","lon":-121.48227,"lat":48.70527,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q37452","url":null},{"id":"node/583376002","name":"Green Lake Falls","kind":"waterfall","lon":-121.49284,"lat":48.69271,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q37438","url":null},{"id":"node/349003529","name":"Austera Peak","kind":"peak","lon":-121.10144,"lat":48.56853,"ele_m":2534.0,"wikidata":"Q14713588","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Austera_Peak","tour":3,"summary":{"extract":"Austera Peak is in North Cascades National Park in the U.S. state of Washington. Located in the south unit of the park, Austera Peak is about 1 mi (1.6 km) north of Klawatti Peak and the same distance south of Primus Peak. The North Klawatti Glacier, Klawatti Glacier and the north section of McAllister Glacier all descend from the slopes of Austera Peak. Austera Peak is the highpoint along an arête known as Austera Towers.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Austera_Peak","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"},"photos":[],"street":null},{"id":"node/348974174","name":"Booker Mountain","kind":"peak","lon":-120.97958,"lat":48.47581,"ele_m":2475.0,"wikidata":"Q19873735","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Booker_Mountain","tour":5,"summary":{"extract":"Booker Mountain, also known as Mount Booker, with an elevation of 8,284 feet (2,525 m), is located in the North Cascades of Washington, about 1.5 miles SE of Mount Buckner, between Park Creek and Stehekin River. It is named in honor of Booker T. Washington.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Booker_Mountain","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"},"photos":[],"street":null},{"id":"node/349003049","name":"Boston Peak","kind":"peak","lon":-121.036,"lat":48.49543,"ele_m":2711.0,"wikidata":"Q4948023","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wi